      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $15$ | $21$ | $22$ | $23$ | $26$ | $17$ | $16$ |\n\nCalculate ${S}_{xx}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{xx}$ is the sum of the squared deviations of the data ${x}_{i}$ from the mean $\\overset{―}{x},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample mean $\\overset{―}{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{2+3+10+9+6+5+7}{7}=6\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{x}$, and their squares.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $15$ | $21$ | $22$ | $23$ | $26$ | $17$ | $16$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\,$** | $-4$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\\,$** | $16$ | $9$ | $16$ | $9$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{xx}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=16+9+16+9+0+1+1=52.\n$$",
